1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,125
A reference material is bought by
somebody. This is what they see — and,
just as importantly, what they do not.

2
00:00:08,125 --> 00:00:16,650
The catalogue carries only released lots
whose value somebody authorised, each with
its certificate and its expiry.

3
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:25,700
Ordering is priced per unit, against her
own organisation. The quantity cannot
exceed the stock that actually exists.

4
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:32,275
It appears in her orders with its own
number — and nobody else’s orders are
visible to her.

5
00:00:32,275 --> 00:00:40,575
The vault holds the certificate behind
every material on her shelves, and says
loudly when one has been withdrawn.

6
00:00:40,575 --> 00:00:49,500
Each one carries a verification address
she can copy and hand to her own auditor,
who needs no account with the producer at
all.

7
00:00:49,500 --> 00:01:00,475
And a laboratory that qualifies for a
price tier claims it here — a request
somebody on the producer side has to
decide, with their reasoning recorded.
